text:
Mia Wang: Quick UX question — when the left nav is collapsed and a user opens a model from deep link, do we keep the breadcrumb visible or hide it to save vertical space? I'm seeing the deep link lose the workspace filter sometimes :confused:
Omar Khan: I think breadcrumbs are useful for context, especially when people land from an external link. Hiding could be fine if we persist the workspace name in the header. But we need clear expectations for back navigation.
Jamie Lee: @Mia can you share a few common deep link patterns? e.g. is it `/workspaces/:ws/models/:id` vs `/models/:id?ws=`? The routing will change how we preserve UI state.
Mia Wang: Sure — two we use now: `/w/123/models/abc` and the short form `/models/abc?ws=123`. Short form is what marketing uses in emails, long form is internal. Both need to keep filter state if opened in compact nav.
Nora Patel: Accessibility nit: if breadcrumb is visible in compact mode, each crumb must have `aria-current` on the active item and a clear focus style when keyboard-traversed. If we hide breadcrumbs we still need a visible page title announcing context to screen readers.
Omar Khan: Proposal: always include a condensed breadcrumb row in compact nav (single line, smaller type). For deep links, prefer canonical route `/w/:ws/models/:id` and do a 301-style redirect client-side for `/models/:id?ws=` so state hydration is consistent.
Jamie Lee: Love that. Small routing contract to document: canonical path, fallback query param, and a `?preserveNav=true|false` flag for embed scenarios. Also we should avoid changing hash fragments for panel state; prefer query params so server logs show the same resource.
Mia Wang: UX question — condensed breadcrumb should truncate workspace names with ellipsis and show full name on hover. Is hover tooltips acceptable for mobile? probably not.
Nora Patel: Tooltip is fine for desktop. For mobile, tap should open a context sheet with the full path and accessible actions (copy link, open parent). Ensure focus trap and `role=dialog`.
design-bot: Spec updated: https://redwood.design/specs/ctx-switcher-deeplink — added canonical routes + `preserveNav` query. Deploy scheduled for canary tomorrow. :robot_face: 
aria-a11y: Quick a11y checklist added to the spec page: 1) breadcrumb uses nav landmark, 2) active crumb has `aria-current="page"`, 3) truncated text includes `aria-label` with full name, 4) mobile sheet is keyboard operable.
Jamie Lee: One last edge case — bookmarked URLs created before this change that rely on `#panel=insights` should still open the correct panel. Can we map old hash fragments to new `panel` query param during hydration?
Omar Khan: Yes, we'll add a small router migration: if `location.hash` contains `panel=`, set `panel` query param and remove the hash. We'll also emit a one-time analytics event `legacy_hash_migration` so we can track usage.
Mia Wang: Awesome. I'll update the design prototypes and add the condensed breadcrumb visuals and mobile sheet interaction. Thanks everyone :raised_hands: 
Jamie Lee: I'll add the routing contract to the frontend RFC and include the hydration snippet:
```
// on app init
const migrateHash = () => {
  const h = new URLSearchParams(window.location.hash.replace('#',''));
  if (h.get('panel')) {
    const p = h.get('panel');
    const params = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
    params.set('panel', p);
    window.history.replaceState({}, '', `${window.location.pathname}?${params.toString()}`);
    analytics.track('legacy_hash_migration');
  }
}
```
aria-a11y: Also recommend a visual focus indicator for the condensed crumb row — 3px outline with accessible color contrast.
Mia Wang: cool, I'll iterate and post updated frames here tomorrow.
Omar Khan: LGTM :thumbsup:
